    "title": "Multiplying a Column-Vector By a Row-Vector"
  },
  "question": {
    ":question/id": "e1ac5e76-5400-5430-b1c3-a91ce44222ba",
    ":question/problem": "$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 10 & -2\\end{bmatrix}=$\n\n| {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| {{field-4}} | {{field-5}} | {{field-6}} |\n| {{field-7}} | {{field-8}} | {{field-9}} |\n| {{field-10}} | {{field-11}} | {{field-12}} |",
    ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
    ":question/worked-solution": "Here, the first factor is $4\\times 1$ matrix and the second factor is a $1\\times 3$ matrix. \n\nThere is $1$ column in the first matrix and $1$ row in the second matrix, so the product is well defined:\n\n$$\n4\\times \\boxed{\\text{1}}✓\\boxed{\\text{1}}\\times 3=4\\times 3\n$$\n\nComputing the product, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 10 & -2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1\\cdot (-6) & 1\\cdot 10 & 1\\cdot (-2) \\\\ 1\\cdot (-6) & 1\\cdot 10 & 1\\cdot (-2) \\\\ 4\\cdot (-6) & 4\\cdot 10 & 4\\cdot (-2) \\\\ 1\\cdot (-6) & 1\\cdot 10 & 1\\cdot (-2)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 10 & -2 \\\\ -6 & 10 & -2 \\\\ -24 & 40 & -8 \\\\ -6 & 10 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
